{
  "dom_id": "step-q117368",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14195367/assets/b66dea0994329cf63ff08137293efc33fe994a5ae0395d661dc014044a1c0d14.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 6CD & =54 \\\\ CD & =9\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14195367/assets/95e84873b1353e6a04bb4e6e7df5f45c09eb4751b1170d6eaf8a9bcfa4b572de.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 9}{2} \\\\  & =18{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
